From 44a445091d296a34ac4676878e92e5976efb69d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timothy Schmidt <timschmidt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:25:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_wcnss: Fix PD regulator selection on Pronto
 v1/v2

Commit 3dbc90b9c22e ("remoteproc: qcom_wcnss: Fix handling the lack of
PD regulators in v3") identifies Pronto v3 by comparing the number of
power domain names with the number of non-PD regulators. Pronto v1 and
v2 also list two power domain names ("cx", "mx") and one non-PD
regulator ("vddpx"), so they take the v3 branch as well.
wcnss_init_regulators() then requests only the first entry of their
vregs table, vddcx, whatever power domains were attached:

 - with only CX attached (MSM8974), vddmx and vddpx are never requested
   and a dummy vddcx is used instead
 - with CX and MX attached (MSM8916, MSM8939), vddpx is never requested
 - with no domains (old device trees), vddmx and vddpx are never
   requested

The remote processor still boots when these rails happen to be enabled
by someone else, so the problem is easy to miss. On an MSM8226 board
with CX as the only power domain, the driver logs

  qcom-wcnss-pil fb204000.remoteproc: supply vddcx not found, using dummy regulator

and the described MX and PX regulators have no WCNSS consumer.

Pronto v3 is the only variant whose table has no PD regulators. Test
num_pd_vregs directly instead of comparing the two counts.

Fixes: 3dbc90b9c22e ("remoteproc: qcom_wcnss: Fix handling the lack of PD regulators in v3")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
Testing:
 - LG Fx0 (MSM8926, Pronto v2, CX as the only power domain) on 6.18.59,
   which carries the stable backport of 3dbc90b9c22e: before the fix, the
   Pronto device's only regulator supplier is the dummy vddcx and the
   RPM L3/L6 regulators have no WCNSS consumer. With this change applied
   to that kernel, the device consumes L3 as vddmx (1.2875 V vote) and L6
   as vddpx, firmware boots, and a stop/start cycle re-registers Wi-Fi.
 - A host model that extracts every wcnss_data table and the branch
   condition from the source: this base fails 6 of 10 table/attached-PD
   combinations (all Pronto v1/v2), the fixed code passes all 10,
   including the Pronto v3 and Riva cases.
 - W=1 LLVM builds of drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.o for ARM
   (multi_v7_defconfig) and arm64 (defconfig) on this base: no warnings.
Not tested: a kernel built from this maintainer tree on hardware, and
MSM8974, MSM8916 or MSM8939 boards; their behaviour is inferred from
their device trees.

 drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.c | 8 ++------
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.c
index c856a92af..fe64b568b 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_wcnss.c
@@ -445,19 +445,15 @@ static int wcnss_init_regulators(struct qcom_wcnss *wcnss,
 {
 	const struct wcnss_vreg_info *info = data->vregs;
 	struct regulator_bulk_data *bulk;
-	size_t i, possible_pds = 0, num_vregs = data->num_vregs;
+	size_t i, num_vregs = data->num_vregs;
 	int ret;
 
-	for (i = 0; i < WCNSS_MAX_PDS; i++)
-		if (data->pd_names[i])
-			possible_pds++;
-
 	/*
 	 * If attaching the power domains suceeded we can skip requesting
 	 * the regulators for the power domains. For old device trees we need to
 	 * reserve extra space to manage them through the regulator interface.
 	 */
-	if (possible_pds >= num_vregs) {
+	if (!data->num_pd_vregs) {
 		/* Do nothing if vregs do not include PD regulators (pronto-v3) */
 	} else if (wcnss->num_pds) {
 		info += wcnss->num_pds;

